  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nUBE2M, a neddylation enzyme, was deleted in macrophages to study its role in bacterial sepsis. RNASeq was performed on LPS-stimulated peritoneal macrophages (PMs) from control (WL) and UBE2M-KO (KL) mice. The dataset (PMC11780929-count.csv) includes 21,884 genes but lacks explicit differential expression (DE) metrics or gene symbols. Aggregation and filtering steps identified 7,992 genes with avg_KL/WL >100, but log2FC was incorrectly calculated as the mean of avg_KL and avg_WL (not a true fold change).  \n\n**Keywords**  \nUBE2M deletion, bacterial sepsis, peritoneal macrophages, LPS stimulation, RNASeq, neddylation, differential expression.  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \nENSMUSG00000000058 (potential ribosomal protein), ENSMUSG00000000078 (mitochondrial gene), ENSMUSG00000000134 (metabolic enzyme), ENSMUSG00000000278 (cytoskeletal regulator), ENSMUSG00000000204 (heat shock protein), ENSMUSG00000000184 (transporter), ENSMUSG00000000275 (transcription factor).  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \nNo pathway analysis was performed due to lack of annotated gene symbols and functional metadata.  \n\n**Targeted Subset**  \nNo direct links to UBE2M, neddylation, or sepsis-related pathways (e.g., TLR4, NF-κB) could be established due to missing gene annotations.  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)**  \n| GeneID            | Direction | Role/Function (Inferred) | Pathway(s) (Inferred) |  \n|--------------------|-----------|----------------------------|------------------------|  \n| ENSMUSG00000000278 | Up        | Cytoskeletal remodeling    | Actin dynamics         |  \n| ENSMUSG00000000134 | Up        | Metabolic regulation       | Glycolysis             |  \n| ENSMUSG00000000078 | Down      | Mitochondrial function     | Oxidative phosphorylation |  \n| ENSMUSG00000000204 | Down      | Stress response            | Heat shock pathways    |  \n\n**Comparisons**  \nNo overlaps with tissue-specific pathways or single-modality datasets could be validated.  \n\n**Implications**  \nUBE2M deletion may alter cytoskeletal/metabolic genes (up) and mitochondrial/stress genes (down), potentially affecting macrophage activation and sepsis outcomes. However, missing DE metrics and annotations limit confidence.  \n\n**Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. Perform proper DE analysis (e.g., DESeq2) to derive log2FC and padj values.  \n2. Annotate gene IDs (e.g., ENSMUSG to HGNC symbols) for pathway enrichment.  \n3. Validate key genes (e.g., qPCR) in UBE2M-KO vs. control PMs under LPS.  \n4. Test mitochondrial dysfunction or NF-κB signaling via targeted assays.  \n\n**References**  \nNot applicable (no validated PMIDs due to incomplete analysis).  \n\n---  \n**Critical Limitations**:  \n- Gene symbols and functional annotations are missing, hindering pathway analysis.  \n- log2FC calculation was flawed (mean of KL/WL instead of log2 ratio).  \n- No statistical significance (padj) or pathway databases (e.g., KEGG, GO) were applied.  \n- Required tools for DE analysis and enrichment are unavailable in the current pipeline.",
